Customer retention is one of the most critical challenges for subscription-based and service businesses. Losing customers directly impacts revenue, profitability, and long-term growth.
This project performs an end-to-end data analysis to identify the key factors driving customer churn and quantify its financial impact. 
The goal is to generate actionable insights that help businesses improve customer retention strategies and reduce revenue loss.

Business Problem
The company observed a steady decline in active customers over time, leading to reduced recurring revenue.

Key objectives:
Measure overall churn rate
Identify behavioural patterns of churned customers
Determine major drivers influencing churn
Estimate revenue loss caused by churn
Segment customers based on risk and value

Dataset Description
The dataset contains customer behavioural, demographic, and subscription information, including:

Customer demographics (age, gender, region, income)
Subscription and payment details
Usage behaviour metrics
Support interaction history
Satisfaction scores
Revenue indicators
Churn status (target variable)

Business Understanding

Defined key performance indicators:
Churn Rate
Customer Lifetime Value (CLV)
Retention Rate
Revenue Loss Percentage


Data Preparation (SQL)

Performed:
Data backup creation
Data cleaning and type conversions
Missing value checks
Duplicate record validation
Data integrity verification
Generated key KPIs using advanced SQL queries.


Exploratory Data Analysis (Python)

Performed:
Churn distribution analysis
Revenue comparison between churned vs active customers
Behavioural pattern analysis
Correlation analysis
Identified initial churn indicators.


Churn Risk Scoring

Developed a risk scoring system based on:
Customer satisfaction levels
Login activity patterns
Support ticket frequency
Recency of last login

Customers were segmented into:
Low Risk
Medium Risk
High Risk


Key Insights

A significant portion of customers churn after low engagement periods
Customers with lower satisfaction scores show much higher churn probability
High support interaction frequency strongly correlates with churn
Revenue loss is heavily concentrated among medium and high-value customers
